<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">Fig. 6</span>: </span><span id="S4.F6.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">Average score performance across 16 LongBench tasks under varying KV cache budgets.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Experimental Settings</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experimental Models:</span>  We conduct experiments on two widely adopted open-source large language models, namely <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> and <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.3</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, to validate the effectiveness of our method and baselines. Both models adopt grouped-query attention (GQA) to improve decoding efficiency, but differ in their overall design (<span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, Llama uses rotary embeddings; Mistral features sliding-window attention), offering complementary perspectives on KV cache behavior across model families.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baseline Methods:</span>  We compare SABlock with six representative methods.
(1) <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Position-based:</span> StreamingLLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> adopts a sliding-window strategy that retains the initial and most recent tokens.
(2) <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Attention-based:</span> These methods score tokens with attention distributions and can be divided into three categories.
<span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">① Token-level:</span> H<sub id="S4.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_sub">2</sub>O <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> evicts tokens by cumulative attention scores; SnapKV <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> scores tokens using attention scores obtained from an observation window; PyramidKV <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> assigns different cache budgets to each layer.
<span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">② Block-level:</span> ChunkKV <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> groups consecutive tokens into fixed-size blocks.
<span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">③ Sentence-level:</span> SentenceKV <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> preserves entire sentences based on syntactic segmentation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Benchmark Datasets:</span>  We evaluate on two representative long-context benchmarks.
(1) <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LongBench</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> tests a model’s ability to reason over extended contexts across 16 tasks, spanning QA, summarization, code completion, and synthetic reasoning.
(2) <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Needle-in-a-Haystack (NIAH)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> focuses on retrieval and localization of critical information, assessing performance on single-needle, multi-needle, and multi-needle reasoning tasks.
These two benchmarks jointly evaluate semantic comprehension and factual retention, providing a holistic view of KV cache compression performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Performance Metrics:</span>  We report four metrics to comprehensively evaluate each method.
(1) <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LongBench average score</span>: the mean accuracy across all 16 tasks, following the official LongBench evaluation protocol <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, which adopts F1 for QA, Rouge-L for summarization, accuracy for synthetic reasoning, and edit similarity for code generation.
(2) <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">NIAH accuracy</span>: the percentage of correctly retrieved or reasoned needles across different NIAH sub-tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>.
(3) <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Peak memory usage</span>: the GPU memory consumed during decoding.
(4) <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Decoding latency</span>: Token-by-Token (TBT) latency under sequential decoding without batching.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
